en you  know intro to philosophy but intro to  philosophy is a little bit different  because  garth wanted us to get teaching  experience so he built it into the  syllabus that two of the lectures  in the course of the semester would be  given by each ta so  you had to present to this massive  auditorium what he was doing so i did  that and then  after a while  they would trust you with your own class  and they began by giving me  sections of ethics to teach and you'd  have to pick out your textbook and  arrange your syllabus  and figure out how you were going to do  assignments you'd have a little bit of  guidance if you asked for it but  otherwise you're kind of just thrown  into it and i taught ethics twice  beginning  in 1999  and then um  i was slated to teach a higher level  class called philosophy of politics law  and justice and i thought that was so  cool  around that time my my mother died and i  began having to go up to milwaukee to  handle her  funeral and arrangements and estate and  house and all that sort of stuff and  then come back so  that class i actually had a few of my my  you know fellow classmates take over  sections but i saw it through to the end  and then i didn't teach for a little  while because i got a dissertation  research award to work on things but i  already had three  of my own classes under my belt at that  time  my last semester there  um i  was kind of fortunate in that i'd run  out of funding by that time  so i i had the inheritance that i could  draw upon that and i began teaching  for  southeastern illinois college which was  about maybe 45 minutes down the road  from carbondale going due east i forget  which town it's in but it's a community  college and so  uh i i knew one of my my friends who was  in the english department his wife was a  professor there and she was like hey we  need somebody to teach philosophy  classes do you think you want to do it  and i was like yeah sounds good so i  would ride in with with her we arranged  our schedules so they worked out well  i think  maybe not every day but but most days  and  then i would teach  twice a week  introduction to philosophy  ethics  and world religions and this is great  because it was giving me  more  staple classes that you need and i only  did that for a semester i you know  finished my dissertation graduated  and then we moved to northwestern  indiana and that's where the the second  part of the story begins  my family has had land in northwestern  indiana near  demotte and rose lawn  off of highway 10 since the 1930s and  there's a lot to say about that maybe  i'll talk about that somewhere else it's  a quite interesting story suffice it to  say there's a you could call it a  compound shalem reeves right the the  lamry's place and there's three houses  there that my grandparents and  great aunts and great  uncles built as retirement homes  and by the time that i was finishing up  graduate school my grandmother and  grandpa were still alive my aunt tony  was still alive but she was suffering  from alzheimer's  and had basically moved out of her house  in with my aunt bibian  who was a nurse and they were they were  essentially taking care of her my  grandfather was also suffering from  alzheimer's and eventually had to be put  in a retirement home but um  you know  at that time i was heavily involved my  family  this is my mother's side of the family  and by that time i was also married i  had  my oldest daughter you know she was born  in 2002 and so by the time i'm  graduating i'm a family man and i have  to think about where i'm going to go so  what often happens with academics is  they say well i'll go wherever the jobs  are and i thought about that and i was  like  i really want to be where my family is  i've got these connections throughout my  life to this land wouldn't it be nice to  move in there so i used what was left of  my inheritance to buy  one of the houses and we moved in and  then the question was okay what am i  going to do for work  so i sent out  cover letters and cvs to  every  college university that offered  philosophy classes within what i thought  to be  a  two-hour driving radius  and i was like well we'll see what  happens and i got some some responses  right away  nor uh  indiana university  northwest based up in gary asked me if i  could teach a professional ethics class  for them which was actually at a high  school because part of continuing  education rather than on the main campus  manchester college way out  in  eastern  indiana about two hours drive booked me  in to teach two ethics classes and so i  was like well  looks like the this will be  more or less it for the time being i did  interview  for lewis university now that i recall  and they wanted people to teach like in  the the chicagoland area  so that was a possibility and then i got  a call from ball state university and it  was from the the chair of the philosophy  and religious studies department julie  eflin and she said hey um  this is a little bit unusual  but do you think you would be interested  in teaching up in michigan city  at indiana state prison we have a  four-year  degree program prison education the  prisoners don't get a philosophy degree  but they can minor in philosophy or  religious studies which by the way you  have to teach both of those and they get  a degree in general studies and you know  the guy who was working up at indiana  state prison he's leaving all of a  sudden do you think you'd want to  take his place and i was like  what's what's the deal well you'll be an  assistant professor you're you're a  member of the department  and this will be like your full-time gig  and i thought about it and i was like  how many classes am i going to teach he  said well  he's slated for five  and i was like wow i've already got  three classes elsewhere and  the five is this doable and i was like  well maybe for a semester it is i'll  i'll try it out right  so that was a very intense semester i  stepped into the classes that he  had scheduled which included religion in  american life intro to philosophy  world religions  jesus and the gospel traditions which  was an upper level class and then there  was there was another maybe there were  two sections of of religion in american  culture so i had a lot of preps a lot of  different things to do  and  after that semester i didn't teach for  indiana university northwest anymore or  for manchester college  i just taught for ball state in their  prison program and i will say this it  was very  tough but very rewarding i mean  physically you know the place was filled  with mold so my allergies were killing  me i had to get uh inserts for my my  shoes because walking on concrete all  the time was kind of rough  you were constantly having to shout  above hvac and fans and stuff like that  my vocal cords would get so strained by  the way the classes were all  uh anywhere from two to three hours  depending on the prison schedule  once a week and so like mondays i would  teach three classes with a break in  between and i could be teaching anywhere  from you know seven to nine hours where  i'm talking the entire time so i would  you know bring in ricola and and sip hot  tea and stuff like that and it was it  was great you know it it gave me an  academic position i was full-time i  unfortunately as i found out later  i was not eligible  because i was in extended education for  tenure or promotion because my chair  told me look at all these publications  you've got we would be promoting you to  associate professor but my hands are  tight but it was a job and i really  enjoyed you know talking with the  students and the the  freedom to teach the things that i  wanted to to teach  gave me a huge vast background  in teaching a whole bunch of philosophy  classes and religious studies classes  and it lasted for six years  unfortunately  um the state of indiana in 2008  very suddenly decided to  make the prisoners ineligible for the  low-income student grant that they were  getting it was always rather unpopular  politically although it was a you know  prison education is is very good for  reducing the recidivism rate the rate of  people going back to  prison committing further crimes  it's one of the most cost-effective  measures we actually have but indiana is  you know a red state and they were  against that that sort of thing so they  finally got rid of it so i had to get on  the job market  very very late i had been interviewing  for other jobs along the way while i was  at indiana  state prison teaching there you know i  had a  catholic studies job up at university  illinois of chicago that i was in the  running for i got to the top four  interviewed didn't get it i went out to  steubenville  interviewed there there were other  interviews as well but i i didn't get  them  and so um  in late to  2007 i had to get on the job market  a little bit behind everybody else and  that's how i wound up in the  the second  academic  position  that i would take down in north carolina  the way people now get hired in the  academic job market is a bit different  than the way it used to be back then you  know sometimes  back then you could have a skype  interview although that was pretty much  a rarity and it depended how tech savvy  the department was generally you had to  go to the american philosophical  association which was in between  christmas and new year's terrible time  of the year  and do your initial interviews there  with all the other people  in you know like a giant room  and the people doing the interviews that  have to do like you know anywhere from  10 to 18 interviews over a couple days  and then it would go to the next thing  you'd wait for like an email or a phone  call you know we'd like to do a  follow-up can you come to campus to do  an interview and that sort of thing  and i i had a couple um interviews at  the apa including with ball state  one of the professors was actually  pretty hostile to the writing sample  that i'd submitted  kind of a jerk actually  didn't go any further with that  i  did get an interview with saint anselm's  college where i was giving the saint  anselm lecture  that year so they kind of rolled those  in together and the people that are  really into anselm were really into me  and i think a lot of the other people  were not the department was in a  considerable flux  i did get two  job offers and  one was locally it was at a place that  if i remember correctly was  in or near joliet so about 45 minutes  drive and it was a community college  and i was going to be you know teaching  philosophy and religious studies classes  um and it's interesting because that  would have been a union position  and like guaranteed pay raises and stuff  like that so this is 2008 remember that  that date the other was at fayetteville  state university in north carolina  and it was for a mixed department  called government and history which  included some philosophy people but it  didn't have a philosophy major  and basically what you were doing is  teaching critical thinking classes but  you know they offered more money uh it  was a university so the you know the  worry was  and this is a well-founded worry if i  went to that community college  it might be very difficult to ever get  out of there and get another position  again because you know a lot of hiring  is based not just on where you went to  going to southern illinois university as  opposed to going to university of  chicago it puts you at a disadvantage  publications even those things outside  lots of publications  um but where you're currently teaching  can also be viewed as you know a feather  in the cap or as a mark against you  so i thought well we'll i'll i'll do  this i'll go to southern i'll go down to  the south to north carolina take up this  position here at this historically black  university  and it'll be going like like going on  military 